Writing YAML relies on indentation, key-value pairs, and dashes to organize data into a clean, readable hierarchy without brackets. Every level of nesting uses spaces (typically two spaces per level) to show which items belong together.

1. The Golden Rules of YAML Formatting

Before writing your first line, keep these strict formatting rules in mind.
• Spaces Only: Always use spaces for indentation, and never press the Tab key.
• Consistent Indentation: Stick to the same number of spaces (2 spaces is the industry standard) for every nested level.
• Space After Colons: Always put a space after a colon in a key-value pair (name: Alice, not name:Alice).
• Case Sensitivity: Keys are case-sensitive, so User and user are treated as two different items.



2. Basic Building Blocks


Key-Value Pairs (Dictionaries/Maps)

The foundation of YAML is a key followed by a colon, a space, and a value.
app_name: MyWebApp
version: 1.0
is_active: true


Nested Objects (Hierarchy)

To group settings under a main category, leave the main key empty and indent the child items on the next lines.
database:
  host: 127.0.0.1
  port: 5432
  username: admin


Lists (Arrays/Sequences)

Use a hyphen followed by a space (- ) to create a list of items.
allowed_roles:
  - admin
  - editor
  - viewer


Lists of Objects

You can combine lists and key-value pairs to define multiple complex items, such as a list of users or servers.
list of dictionaries
users:
  - name: Alice
    role: admin
  - name: Bob
    role: editor


3. Data Types & Multi-line Text


Common Data Types

YAML automatically detects most data types without needing quotes.
• Strings: greeting: Hello World (Use quotes if the text contains special characters like colons: message: "Error: File not found").
• Numbers: retries: 3 or timeout: 2.5.
• Booleans: debug: true or cache: false.
• Null values: middle_name: null or middle_name: ~.


Multi-line Strings

When you need to write a long block of text or a script, use a pipe (|) or a greater-than sign (>).
• Preserve Newlines (|): Keeps every line break exactly as you typed it.

startup_script: |
  echo "Starting server..."
  npm install
  npm start

• Fold Newlines (>): Joins multiple lines into a single paragraph separated by spaces.

description: >
  This text spans multiple lines in the file
  but will be read as one continuous sentence.



4. Putting It All Together

Here is a complete, realistic configuration file combining everything above.

# Application Configuration File
# Nested Dictionaries
service:
  name: payment-gateway
  environment: production
  port: 8080
  debug: false

# List of supported currencies
currencies:
  - INR
  - USD
  - EUR

# Database connections
databases:
  - type: primary
    host: ://example.com
    replicas: 2
  - type: backup
    host: ://example.com
    replicas: 1


yaml is a dictionary

List of dictionaries: Start the first key of each dictionary with a dash (- ) and align the remaining keys beneath it.

employees:
  - id: 101
    role: Backend Engineer
  - id: 102
    role: DevOps Engineer

